      },
      "rev_n_1_979e70_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_1_979e70_0",
        "node_id": "n_1_979e70",
        "thought_text": "Sort the cards in descending order and have Alice take the largest available card, then Bob take the next largest, and so on. This greedy approach works because taking the largest available number always maximizes the immediate gain and denies the opponent the largest possible value, which is optimal for this specific game structure.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507988.945875
      },
      "rev_n_2_4c301a_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_2_4c301a_0",
        "node_id": "n_2_4c301a",
        "thought_text": "Model the game as a minimax problem where each player tries to maximize their own score minus the opponent's score. While theoretically sound, for N up to 100, the state space is too large for full minimax without memoization, and the greedy strategy is known to be equivalent to the optimal solution for this specific 'take any card' variant, making complex recursion unnecessary.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507988.9458895
      },
      "rev_n_3_5bb07c_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_3_5bb07c_0",
        "node_id": "n_3_5bb07c",
        "thought_text": "Simulate the game by having Alice pick the card with the maximum value among remaining cards, and Bob do the same. Calculate the final difference. This is effectively the same as the sorting strategy but described as a step-by-step simulation process to verify the logic holds for small N before generalizing.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "root",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794507988.945897
      },
      "rev_n_4_4d63af_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_4_4d63af_0",
        "node_id": "n_4_4d63af",
        "thought_text": "Sort the cards in descending order and have Alice take the largest available card, then Bob take the next largest, and so on. This greedy approach works because taking the largest available number always maximizes the immediate gain and denies the opponent the largest possible value, which is optimal for this specific game structure.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "298475169170055244",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794508004.3884103
      },
      "rev_n_5_655a91_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_5_655a91_0",
        "node_id": "n_5_655a91",
        "thought_text": "Model the game as a zero-sum game where the payoff is Alice's score minus Bob's score. Since the game is finite, perfect information, and has no possibility of a draw, the minimax theorem guarantees that the optimal strategy can be found by recursively evaluating all possible moves, though this is computationally expensive for large N.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "298475169170055244",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794508004.3884258
      },
      "rev_n_6_e55690_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_6_e55690_0",
        "node_id": "n_6_e55690",
        "thought_text": "Consider dynamic programming where the state is defined by the set of remaining cards. The value of a state is the maximum difference the current player can achieve from that state. While theoretically sound, the state space size (2^N) makes this approach infeasible for N up to 100, confirming that the greedy strategy is the only practical solution.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "298475169170055244",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794508004.3884346
      },
      "rev_n_7_f2f758_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_7_f2f758_0",
        "node_id": "n_7_f2f758",
        "thought_text": "Sort the cards in descending order and have Alice take the largest available card, then Bob take the next largest, and so on. This greedy approach works because taking the largest available number always maximizes the immediate gain and denies the opponent the largest possible value, which is optimal for this specific game structure.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-5942248611748398195",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794508021.1750555
      },
      "rev_n_8_1a52b6_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_8_1a52b6_0",
        "node_id": "n_8_1a52b6",
        "thought_text": "Model the game as a zero-sum game where the payoff is (Alice's sum - Bob's sum). Since the total sum is constant, maximizing one's own sum is equivalent to maximizing the difference. The optimal strategy for both players is to always pick the maximum available number, leading to the same sorted distribution as the greedy approach.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-5942248611748398195",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794508021.175073
      },
      "rev_n_9_77ba52_0": {
        "revision_id": "rev_n_9_77ba52_0",
        "node_id": "n_9_77ba52",
        "thought_text": "Verify the greedy hypothesis by induction: assume the strategy holds for N-1 cards. For N cards, if a player does not pick the maximum card M, the opponent can pick M in the next turn, resulting in a strictly worse outcome for the first player compared to picking M immediately, confirming that picking the maximum is always the dominant strategy.",
        "parent_revision_path_hash": "-5942248611748398195",
        "replaces_revision_id": null,
        "feedback_id": null,
        "created_at": 1794508021.1750813
      },
